The test uses actual Target Worker runtimes and two RocksDB Shard Stores for the same Target ID. The replacement Shard replays a previously committed Command at its next source position. Its Shard mutation sequence advances once, the injected ACK callback returns UNKNOWN, and the exact entry remains pending. The next fleet turn applies a duplicate previously committed Command on the sibling Shard; its source mutation sequence advances once and its ACK callback also returns UNKNOWN. Both runtimes remain unfenced with independent pending entries. After the replacement Shard's test Owner lease is released, the next fleet turn retries only its pending ACK: the runtime fences, retains the exact entry, does not invoke the ACK callback again, and makes no Store mutation. The following fleet turn still reaches the sibling Shard, which confirms its pending ACK and clears its entry without another Store mutation. Both source operations are duplicate replays and do not send new business work.

Evidence boundary: source, ACK, and Owner lease are test fixtures; lease loss uses the in-memory authority. This verifies local Worker/Store source-apply interleaving, per-Shard fencing at ACK retry, preservation of the fenced Shard's pending obligation, and continued sibling ACK progress. It does not verify live Oxia fencing, real Broker ACK response loss, Claim/Admission/Producer ownership failures, uncertain Store writes, new sibling business work, process termination, shared-channel teardown, or centralized validation. D2 remains IN_PROGRESS / PENDING_CENTRAL_VALIDATION.

